#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483510} :activity/id #uuid "793ea116-b8fe-55b9-b26d-bb211f1fd415" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483438 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "042a3167-88c1-58d7-9cb9-db2e59913bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡1-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445782} {:answer/id #uuid "0bcb67a6-a89e-5cb5-bf50-d01668c64856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡1-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445783} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fc5106-7c16-50e1-afac-b55e9efdb392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445804} {:answer/id #uuid "c56a9809-f9cb-5e51-a1c8-7aa478b44cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445805} {:answer/id #uuid "f647841e-742e-5179-a5c3-3b9852200fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445814} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7552f1e-9180-5b58-ac47-de556c1b13e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7dc3d2ae-4ebe-55e5-a13e-304e391443f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-81347" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dy\\,dx=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡y}{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡0}{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "118abe47-2b28-5f68-9db7-3d02dd65e766" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483469}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483442 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193ddeed-a793-53c6-ba1e-92e68b0f73f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424198} {:answer/id #uuid "4849233c-e677-553e-b525-8ce1ed84e89f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424203} {:answer/id #uuid "6e36ebd6-f283-5b52-b5a9-f667a2f4b389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424209} {:answer/id #uuid "cd60ac0d-9b78-5c69-bd7b-06d4c14bdb66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424219} {:answer/id #uuid "f47d0d8f-c2bd-587b-8969-e8a2b85184c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424220}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424203} :answer-field/id #uuid "3452daaf-b0f7-51b4-a94c-d00ee7b3816f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "df216990-7a71-5841-8a5b-955dc6939d8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-123196" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(x+y)^{2}\\,i+(y+z)^{2}\\,j+(x+z)^{2}\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+z)^{2}) \\\\  & =2(x+y)+2(y+z)+2(x+z) \\\\  & =4(x+y+z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,1,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-2,1,-1) & =4((-2)+(1)+(-1)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1b35f51c-e940-5ca1-a6eb-b4d04e88b3d2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483438}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256606 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd220d6-2d57-5a20-9815-41cc1315dfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253762} {:answer/id #uuid "278a18b4-76f4-5e4b-9a16-d80569c58fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254021} {:answer/id #uuid "2b60c09e-22e6-5285-b087-6989d01f0023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254056} {:answer/id #uuid "391f4af1-1c5a-5f46-ba26-d2d5f64442b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254167} {:answer/id #uuid "eff951cd-1bad-5ebc-a893-9192385cbff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254167} :answer-field/id #uuid "420d9ebd-fd22-53d2-b5da-7a6057798e29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "87e41470-a296-5303-9038-b8074f8d3617" :question/math-academy-id "q-122315" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(3{e}^{x}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/78/785776cee4f82c5bfa5e86b5b1cb746b7c80f2d702fb52f67055a60bdff249ae.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(3{e}^{x}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=3{e}^{x}+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3{e}^{x}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5d7b0892-cfd1-5b51-a2ff-c52c3605d29f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483491}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483469 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32db9a19-c854-5520-a7b1-2e2d6cf6e199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482901} {:answer/id #uuid "76b2864e-9b21-58b8-adab-d939746fcd0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483020} {:answer/id #uuid "89711884-fd84-5d40-b578-3b26b6db7773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483052} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e747ad-06a7-5c00-a1b9-ff6605fffbeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483121} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3e0303-17c8-5f8d-9593-625d918333fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483209} :answer-field/id #uuid "5067a1f1-560a-544d-98c4-afd94a6e74d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1430584e-5e6b-5a21-98c2-3433956718bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-110936" :question/problem "|  | $Y=3$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ |\n\nCompute $P((X,Y)\\in \\{(2,3),(2,6)\\})$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P((X,Y)\\in \\{(2,3),(2,6)\\}) & =P((X,Y)=(2,3))+P((X,Y)=(2,6)) \\\\  & =f(2,3)+f(2,6) \\\\  & =0.25+0.5 \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6f8e4396-102c-5e78-8d1e-683b8f19f689"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0275b76b-d677-5923-822e-d4a297264f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482813} {:answer/id #uuid "6c21ffc6-65d5-53cb-861b-5bdbe9419a54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483000} {:answer/id #uuid "7b415212-1d06-525d-8740-dee3d770e84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483027} {:answer/id #uuid "ace2689e-b272-51e6-9126-9b1901cffc10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483105} {:answer/id #uuid "ec0b3590-3a88-5482-8b18-951f43340352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483208}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483000} :answer-field/id #uuid "da8e8c09-3017-56e6-90da-5157ba9484f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d215478d-b523-59e2-bf03-ca21eb9b301e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71571" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=z-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3 & =3 \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=3$ is the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "adca254e-ec27-5cd2-80b9-9701e67b2101" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483442}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483491 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56f3cc0b-c41a-5321-9ece-52fd3df64297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482965} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6d1a51-1150-5aa3-a923-b68b4f05b638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483003} {:answer/id #uuid "704a8b44-8729-5565-b1a0-2928425f981b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483007} {:answer/id #uuid "c19dca01-b95f-5b0c-9166-3c0789c3ce6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483137} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcdefc7-8d2b-5c46-b8de-33fa51d9e81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483241}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483137} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd91fbcc-9a1e-515e-941d-2e16f7668942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5c8f03a1-964a-5fd4-a60f-a2aa229186e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-43048" :question/problem "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $A$ and $B$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(A)=6$, find $det(B).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ can be obtained from $A$ by swapping the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(6) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "babb3eac-e398-52e9-a29c-dae5040a1cb9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483487}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483510 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e163fd4-f82d-598e-bb46-e9eb87f8d20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3xe^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482974}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482974} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a4120a4-2ac8-5351-bb5f-79cfef062572" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "178b0d68-b124-5a5b-940e-16d447e5211f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332691" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=3{e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $3{e}^{-5x},$ and ${e}^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x})+5\\alpha x{e}^{-5x} & =3{e}^{-5x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =3{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =3{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x{e}^{-5x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fedc8bb3-4406-5d39-8711-ea07b719c253" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483461}}] :activity/title "Quiz 12" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482807}]}
